{
 "id": "sunk-cost",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE SUNK COST FALLACY",
 "angle": "Why do we keep going with things we've paid for, even when they make us miserable?",
 "answer": "Because wasting what we've already spent feels like a loss: people pick the dearer, worse trip and full-price ticket holders attend more plays; the same pull shows up in mice and rats, so it is older than money; the rational rule is that only future costs and benefits count.",
 "logline": "Most people chose the ski trip they would enjoy less because it cost more, so why? Ohio psychologists found over half picked the $100 trip over the better $50 one, but that was hypothetical, so they discounted real theatre tickets and full-price buyers went to more plays, but it isn't just human: in 2018 mice, rats and people all kept waiting longer the more they'd waited, but biologists named it after Concorde, so the cure is simple: only the future counts.",
 "hook_text": "THE TRIP THEY'D ENJOY LESS",
 "hooks": [
  "Mice, rats and people all fall for the same money mistake.",
  "Full-price theatre ticket buyers went to more plays. Here's why that's irrational."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "default"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "In a 1985 study, most people chose the holiday they'd enjoy less.",
   "say": "In a nineteen eighty-five study, most people chose the holiday they'd enjoy less.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "It had cost them more.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "So why do we keep paying for things that make us miserable?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Ohio University psychologists posed a puzzle: you've booked two ski trips for one weekend.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Michigan cost 100 dollars; Wisconsin cost 50, and sounds more fun.",
   "say": "Michigan cost a hundred dollars; Wisconsin cost fifty, and sounds more fun.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Over half chose Michigan, not to waste the bigger sum.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But that was only imaginary money.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "So they sold real theatre season tickets, giving random buyers a discount.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Full-price buyers went to more plays, at least for half a season.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Economists call it the sunk cost fallacy: money already gone shouldn't change your next move.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But it isn't just a human quirk.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 2018, scientists gave mice, rats and people the same waiting game.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail",
   "say": "In twenty eighteen, scientists gave mice, rats and people the same waiting game."
  },
  {
   "text": "All three were likelier to keep waiting, the longer they had already waited.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the pull of a sunk cost may be older than money itself.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But biologists named this mistake after a jet.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Britain and France kept funding Concorde long after it couldn't pay off; only fourteen flew passengers.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the cure is easy to say, hard to do: only the future counts.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "What are you still doing just because you've already paid for it?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
